markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Facilitating Prediction Models

## What's Actually Happening in This Lesson

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">1</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Navigating Uncertainty warm-up:</strong> students brainstorm questions Anita's Parlor faces, kept visible for the whole lesson</div>
  </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">2</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Regression and Classification widgets (Levels 1-5):</strong> students tinker with both, then Level 5 asks them to label scenarios as one or the other, the real formative assessment</div>
  </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">3</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Wrap-up:</strong> the class revisits every question from the warm-up, one at a time, deciding together whether regression or classification fits</div>
  </div>
</div>

<hr>

## Watch for: Mislabeling Classification vs. Regression

<div style="display: flex; gap: 16px; margin: 20px 0; flex-wrap: wrap;">
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background-color: #FFE3CE; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; color: #611D00; margin-bottom: 10px;">Stuck: Guesses from Habit</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #611D00; line-height: 1.6;">Calls "how many scoops will sell tomorrow?" classification, because it feels like a prediction, without checking what the output actually is.</div>
  </div>
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background-color: #CCF1D0; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; color: #003F25; margin-bottom: 10px;">Strong: Checks the Output First</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #003F25; line-height: 1.6;">"The answer is a number of scoops, so that's regression. If it asked yes-or-no whether we'll sell out, that'd be classification."</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Language to borrow:</strong>
  </div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222;">&bull; "Don't think about whether it's hard or easy to predict, just ask: is the answer a category, or a number?"</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 18px; margin-top: 20px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #06338D; line-height: 1.6;">
    <strong>&#8505; A facilitation move worth planning for:</strong> the lesson's own wrap-up technique is to go through the brainstormed questions one at a time, not all at once, asking the whole class to vote regression or classification before moving to the next. Mistakes here are explicitly meant to be used as teaching moments, not corrected and skipped past.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
